          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which virulence factor of Streptococcus pyogenes is responsible for both evading</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which virulence factor of Streptococcus pyogenes is responsible for both evading phagocytosis and triggering rheumatic fever through molecular mimicry with cardiac myosin?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Streptolysin O</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Streptolysin O lyses red blood cells and leucocytes and serves as a serological marker (ASO titre), but it does not share epitopes with cardiac tissue.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>M protein</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>M protein is anti-phagocytic (prevents opsonisation) and shares structural epitopes with cardiac myosin, so an immune response against it cross-reacts with heart tissue, causing rheumatic fever.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hyaluronidase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hyaluronidase degrades connective tissue hyaluronic acid to aid bacterial spread, but it plays no role in molecular mimicry or immune evasion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin (SPE)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>SPE acts as a superantigen causing scarlet fever and streptococcal toxic shock syndrome, not autoimmune cardiac damage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the virulence factors: M protein is the key factor that both prevents phagocytosis and, through molecular mimicry with cardiac myosin, triggers the autoimmune damage of rheumatic fever."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. M protein's dual role — anti-phagocytic shield on the bacterial surface and molecular mimic of cardiac myosin — is why untreated GAS pharyngitis can lead to rheumatic fever 2–4 weeks later."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
