            "from": 341.675,
            "to": 351.675
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In primary pulmonary tuberculosis, the Ghon complex consists of which combinatio</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In primary pulmonary tuberculosis, the Ghon complex consists of which combination?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Subpleural parenchymal focus (Ghon focus) and ipsilateral hilar lymph node involvement</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The Ghon complex = Ghon focus (subpleural, usually lower-zone parenchymal lesion) + draining hilar lymph node with granulomatous inflammation. Most primary TB is self-healing at this stage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Upper-lobe cavitary lesion and pleural effusion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Upper-lobe cavitation is the hallmark of reactivation (secondary) TB, not primary TB. The Ghon focus is typically subpleural in the lower or middle zones.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Miliary nodules scattered across both lung fields</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Miliary TB results from haematogenous dissemination of bacilli, which is a complication of primary or reactivation TB, not the Ghon complex itself.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bronchopneumonic consolidation with empyema</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bronchopneumonia with empyema is more typical of pyogenic bacterial pneumonia (e.g., Staphylococcus aureus). Primary TB forms a granulomatous focus, not suppurative consolidation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: the Ghon complex = Ghon focus (subpleural parenchymal lesion) + ipsilateral hilar lymph node. Upper-lobe cavitation belongs to reactivation TB, not primary infection."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The Ghon complex — parenchymal Ghon focus plus involved hilar lymph node — is the pathological signature of primary TB and usually self-heals with calcification."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
