        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 518.325,
            "to": 528.325
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A child presents with fever, sore throat, and a greyish-white pseudomembrane on </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A child presents with fever, sore throat, and a greyish-white pseudomembrane on the tonsils that bleeds when touched. Which stain on a throat swab smear will best help confirm the suspected diagnosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Gram stain</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Gram stain shows Gram-positive bacilli but cannot demonstrate the characteristic metachromatic (volutin) granules that identify Corynebacterium diphtheriae. It is a screening stain, not confirmatory for diphtheria.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ziehl–Neelsen stain</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>ZN stain detects acid-fast bacilli such as Mycobacterium tuberculosis. It has no role in suspected diphtheria.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Albert stain</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Albert stain demonstrates blue-black metachromatic granules within yellowish-green bacilli, characteristic of C. diphtheriae — the confirmatory stain before starting antitoxin therapy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>KOH mount</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>KOH mount is used to demonstrate pseudohyphae and budding yeast cells of Candida in suspected oral candidiasis, not for bacterial pharyngitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In suspected diphtheria (pseudomembrane on tonsils), Albert stain is the confirmatory stain — it shows blue-black metachromatic granules in yellowish-green bacilli, identifying C. diphtheriae."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Albert stain is the key stain for diphtheria — it reveals the diagnostic metachromatic (volutin) granules of C. diphtheriae, guiding early antitoxin therapy even before culture results."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
